  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 PM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nThis Afternoon: Storm Details\n\nSeveral rounds of storms have developed since sunrise on the\nnose of a 850-700-mb theta-e ridge that is advancing eastward\njust ahead of a surface cold front bifurcating Minnesota from \nSW to NE. As expected, the complex hodographs and linear \nforcing, coupled with a narrow instability axis in which storms \nhave little residence time, have resulted in transient storm \nstructures and a rapid upscale growth. The southern side of the \nline has become the dominant feature as of 18Z and is making the\nsoutheastward turn as the cold pool becomes established. This \ntrend will continue over the next few hours, with damaging winds\nbeing the main threat. \n\nAdditional storms may form along the nose the the theta-e ridge,\nbut time is running out for this to occur before the cold front\narrives. The cold front may serve as a source for additional\nconvective initiation, but there has been ample anvil shading\nalong the front and any risk for these storms may be displaced\nfurther north towards northern Wisconsin.\n\n\nSunday Morning: Next Round of Storms, Heavy Rain Threat?\n\nCooler and more stable air filters southward tonight in the wake of \nthe cold front and persists for much of Saturday as the front stalls \nand pivots near I-80 in Iowa. Lee cyclogenesis along the Front Range \nhelps push this (now warm) front northward across the region once \nagain late Saturday night into Sunday morning. Aided by a 30-40 kt \nLLJ, these storms could be more robust in nature and produce heavy \nrainfall. While confidence is increasing that there will be some \nsort of MCS across the region, the devil is in the details as to \nwhen exactly it will form, which will dictate its track. Aided by \nincreasing Gulf moisture and PWATs of 1.5 to 2 inches, coupled with \nan increasing line parallel storm motion, this complex could put \ndown efficient rainfall rates in short order and will need to be \nmonitored for any flooding risk. \n\n\nEarly Next Week: Continued Storm Risk\n\nThe lower tropospheric front stalls between central Iowa and \nsouthern Minnesota for later Sunday through Tuesday, presenting \nmultiple windows of opportunities for convection that could \nbring heavy rain. However, details of these storms remain very \nnebulous and will be refined over the coming days.\n\n\nMid to Late Week: Drier and Cooler\n\nThe medium to longer range solutions are in general agreement \nthat a a polar airmass will build southward through mid to late \nweek as the surface baroclinic zone sags southward. This should \nresult in cooler and drier weather, though with the surface \nfront not far away, it will not take much of a wobble in the \nforecast to bring this rain back northward.",
